    {
      "id": "be-s12m-perfect-competition-c1",
      "heading": "The environment and its implications",
      "explanation_md": "Picture a vegetable market where every stall sells identical tomatoes and every buyer can see every price. If one seller charges more, buyers simply walk to the next stall.\n\nThat is **perfect competition\\**: **many buyers and sellers\\**, an **identical product\\**, **perfect information\\**, **no transaction costs\\** and **free entry and exit\\**.\n\nThree things follow:\n\n- Each firm is a **price taker\\**, so the price it gets for one more unit is the market price: **P = MR\\**.\n- In the **short run\\** a firm can make a **profit or a loss\\**.\n- In the **long run\\**, entry and exit push **profit to zero\\**.\n\nWhy study something so rare? It is the **benchmark\\** every other market is compared with, and it shows managers why **differentiation\\** matters.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Many firms, identical product, perfect information, no transaction costs, free entry: price takers with zero long-run profit.",
